## Permitted Purposes

Any purpose is a permitted purpose, commercial use included, except as the Sanctions section limits it.

## Where Copies Come From

You may get the software only from the licensor's own channels: the releases at https://github.com/Veritwin/token-swap-cli, the npm package @veritwin/cli, or another channel the licensor names in writing. Each machine that runs the software must get its copy from one of those channels. You may not give copies to anyone else, others in your company included, and you may not keep copies for others to use, such as in shared images, caches or mirrors.

## The Service

The software is a client of the licensor's token swap service. These terms give you no right to use that service. Using it needs an api key from the licensor, under the terms the licensor sets for the service.

## Your Keys and Transactions

The software holds no keys. You sign every transaction yourself, in your own wallet or with your own program, and you are responsible for every transaction you sign. Transactions on a blockchain can't be undone.

## Pre-release Versions

Versions whose number has a suffix, such as -alpha or -dev, are test versions. They work with test networks, and they may change or stop working at any time, without notice.

## Update Checks

When it runs at a terminal, the software asks, at most once a day, for the newest version of its release type: the npm registry, or GitHub for -dev versions. It does this to tell you when an update is available. Setting the environment variable VERITWIN_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1 turns this off.

